  "content": {
    "id": "p46-q1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": "Find the points of intersection of these graphs. You will need to rearrange before equating.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p46-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=x^2$ and $x=\\frac{y}{2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,0)$ and $(2,4)$",
          "worked": "Rearrange the second equation: $y=2x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=2x\\\\x^2-2x&=0\\\\x(x-2)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }2\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=0$, $y=2(0)=0$. When $x=2$, $y=2(2)=4$.\nThe points of intersection are $(0,0)$ and $(2,4)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y+4x^2+x-6=0$ and $y-3x=7$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-0.5,5.5)$",
          "worked": "Rearrange the equations: $y=-4x^2-x+6$ and $y=3x+7$.\n$$\\begin{align*}-4x^2-x+6&=3x+7\\\\4x^2+4x+1&=0\\\\(2x+1)^2&=0\\\\x&=-\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=3\\left(-\\frac12\\right)+7\\\\y&=\\frac{11}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nThe point of intersection is $(-0.5,5.5)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=x^2+3x-1$ and $3y=x-3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(-\\frac83,-\\frac{17}{9}\\right)$ and $(0,-1)$",
          "worked": "Rearrange the second equation: $y=\\frac{x-3}{3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+3x-1&=\\frac{x-3}{3}\\\\3x^2+9x-3&=x-3\\\\3x^2+8x&=0\\\\x(3x+8)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }-\\frac83\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=0$, $y=\\frac{0-3}{3}=-1$.\nWhen $x=-\\frac83$, $y=\\frac{-\\frac83-3}{3}=-\\frac{17}{9}$.\nThe points of intersection are $\\left(-\\frac83,-\\frac{17}{9}\\right)$ and $(0,-1)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q1-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$y-7=(x-2)^2$ and $y+x=9$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(1,8)$ and $(2,7)$",
          "worked": "Rearrange the equations: $y=(x-2)^2+7$ and $y=9-x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x-2)^2+7&=9-x\\\\x^2-4x+11&=9-x\\\\x^2-3x+2&=0\\\\(x-1)(x-2)&=0\\\\x&=1\\text{ or }2\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=1$, $y=9-1=8$. When $x=2$, $y=9-2=7$.\nThe points of intersection are $(1,8)$ and $(2,7)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q1-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$xy=1$ and $y=x-1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2},\\frac{-1-\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}\\right)$",
          "worked": "Rearrange the first equation: $y=\\frac1x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1x&=x-1\\\\1&=x^2-x\\\\x^2-x-1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt{(-1)^2-4(1)(-1)}}{2}\\\\x&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2}$, $y=\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2}-1=\\frac{-1-\\sqrt5}{2}$.\nWhen $x=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$, $y=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}-1=\\frac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}$.\nThe points of intersection are $\\left(\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2},\\frac{-1-\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q1-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$x^2+y^2=4$ and $2y+x=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(-\\frac4{\\sqrt5},\\frac2{\\sqrt5}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac4{\\sqrt5},-\\frac2{\\sqrt5}\\right)$",
          "worked": "Rearrange the second equation: $y=-\\frac{x}{2}$. Substitute into the circle equation.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+\\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2&=4\\\\x^2+\\frac{x^2}{4}&=4\\\\5x^2&=16\\\\x^2&=\\frac{16}{5}\\\\x&=\\pm\\frac4{\\sqrt5}\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=-\\frac4{\\sqrt5}$, $y=-\\frac12\\left(-\\frac4{\\sqrt5}\\right)=\\frac2{\\sqrt5}$.\nWhen $x=\\frac4{\\sqrt5}$, $y=-\\frac12\\left(\\frac4{\\sqrt5}\\right)=-\\frac2{\\sqrt5}$.\nThe points of intersection are $\\left(-\\frac4{\\sqrt5},\\frac2{\\sqrt5}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac4{\\sqrt5},-\\frac2{\\sqrt5}\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
